package pages

import "github.com/atknatk/tappa/web/templates/components"

// The EMPLOYEES section — the panel's answer to "who works here?", and since M6-05
// phase B the place a manager acts on the answer.
//
// 🔴 IT RENDERS PanelShell, NOT PanelShellWithScript, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SCRIPT
// DECISION. PanelShell has no script slot (user decision, 2026-08-06: the split
// exists so that giving a section a script is a COMPILE error rather than an edit
// to a string), so this section could not load one without naming a different
// component on purpose. It does not need to: paging here is an ordinary link to the
// next page as a whole document, which keeps the panel's widened Content-Security-
// Policy on exactly ONE url — the cardinality
// TestPanelScreens_ScriptsAndPolicyAgreeAndReachNoThirdParty pins. What that costs
// is measured in internal/handler/employees.go.
//
// ⚠️ THE PARAGRAPH HERE SAID "THIS IS A READ AND IT SHOWS NO SECRET … no invite
// button, no deactivate control: those are phase B". Half of it is now false and the
// half that matters is not, so it is split rather than deleted:
//
// 🔴 §4.7 — NOTHING BELOW RENDERS A SECRET, AND THE VIEW MODEL IS WHY. EmployeesView
// has no field for an invitation code, a session token, a device label or an email
// address, so this template could not print one if it tried. The activation link
// reaches exactly ONE template — AdminInviteIssued at the foot of this file, rendered
// by the POST that mints it — and that is the whole of the panel's exposure to a live
// credential.
//
// 🔴 THE CONTROLS ARE HERE NOW, AND THEY ARRIVED WITH THEIR HANDLERS. A screen
// offering something the server does not implement is this repository's most
// expensive class of mistake, and it takes TWO nets because it has two halves — an
// audit measured the second one missing while this comment named only the first:
//
//	does the control lead somewhere SERVED?   TestEmployeesSection_
//	  EveryControlLeadsSomewhereThatExists drives every form target and every link
//	  on this page through the real router.
//	would the server REFUSE what it offers?  TestEmployeesSection_
//	  OffersNoActionTheServerWouldRefuse renders a DEACTIVATED person's card and
//	  requires the two controls the database would refuse to be absent — with the
//	  sentences that explain them present, and an active person's card as the control.
//
// The first says nothing about the second: setting CanInvite and CanDeactivate to a
// constant true left the whole package green (measured, twice, -race).
//
// 🔴 §4.6 — THE EMPTY STATE IS GATED ON HAVING ASKED. "Nobody works here" is a
// claim about a business and the product may not make one it has not measured;
// v.Queried is set only after the database answered, and a failed read renders a
// problem page in the handler rather than an empty list here.
templ AdminEmployees(v EmployeesView) {
	@PanelShell(v.PanelChrome) {
		if v.Done != "" {
			@components.Notice(components.ToneOK, doneRosterHeading(v.Done)) {
				switch v.Done {
					case "deactivated":
						// 🔴 THE SENTENCE IS ABOUT WHAT WAS WRITTEN, AND ABOUT WHAT WAS
						// NOT. Deactivation does not sign a phone out — the refusal comes
						// from the next tap being judged against the person's status — and
						// a manager who believes the phone is dead would stop watching for
						// it. Saying it here is cheaper than the support call.
						<p>
							Their taps will be refused from now on, and every refusal is still
							recorded. Any phone they signed in on stays signed in; it simply
							cannot record time any more.
						</p>
					default:
						if v.Actions != nil {
							<p>{ movedLine(v.Actions) }</p>
						}
				}
			}
		}
		if v.Problem != "" {
			@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, problemRosterHeading(v.Problem)) {
				switch v.Problem {
					case "unreadable":
						<p>We could not read that submission, so nothing was changed.</p>
					case "confirm-required":
						// 🔴 THE TWO SENTENCES THIS GATE OWES THE MANAGER (§4.6). This one
						// covers "never confirmed" and "confirmed too long ago" together,
						// because from the server they are the same absence — the browser
						// stops sending an expired cookie — and telling somebody which of
						// the two it was would be a claim nothing measured.
						<p>Nothing was changed. Deactivating somebody needs the warning screen first, and this one either was not used or was left open too long.</p>
						<p class="mt-1">Open the person again and read the warning; the button appears under it.</p>
					case "confirm-stale":
						<p>Nothing was changed. This browser is in the middle of confirming a different person, so the button you pressed no longer matches.</p>
						<p class="mt-1">Open the person you meant again and confirm from there — one confirmation at a time.</p>
					case "actions-unavailable":
						// 🔴 A DIFFERENT SENTENCE FROM "unreadable", BECAUSE A DIFFERENT
						// THING FAILED. That one is about a REQUEST nobody could parse and
						// says "nothing was looked up"; this one is OUR read failing after
						// the roster answered. Sharing the word made the screen tell a
						// manager their submission was malformed when in fact our database
						// was.
						<p>We could not load this person's details, so no actions are shown. The list below is current.</p>
					case "unknown":
						<p>That person is not on this business's roster. Nothing was changed.</p>
					case "already-deactivated":
						<p>They were already deactivated, so nothing was written and nothing was recorded twice.</p>
					case "unknown-placement":
						// 🔴 TWO CAUSES, ONE SENTENCE, AND BOTH ARE NAMED. The statement
						// refuses a venue or department that is not this business's AND a
						// department that belongs to a DIFFERENT venue — the second was
						// silently accepted until a security audit measured it, and the
						// dropdown still offers every department in the business (grouped by
						// venue, so the pairing is readable). Telling the manager only
						// "not this business's" would send them looking for a permissions
						// problem they do not have.
						<p>
							Nothing was changed. Either that venue or department is not this
							business's, or the department belongs to a different venue — a person's
							department has to be one of the venue they work at.
						</p>
					case "no-change":
						// ⚠️ THE SENTENCE IS TRUE ABOUT THE STATE AND SILENT ABOUT THE
						// CAUSE, deliberately. Two managers saving the same placement at
						// once was measured: 8 concurrent saves produced 4 writes and 4 of
						// these, so the loser of that race reads this after asking for a
						// real change. The product cannot tell the two apart — nothing
						// records what the placement was when the form was rendered — so
						// the second line points at the card rather than guessing.
						<p>That is already where they work, so there was nothing to save.</p>
						<p class="mt-1">If you had just changed it, check the placement above — a save you made a moment ago, or somebody else's, may already have applied it.</p>
					default:
						<p>
							An activation link can only be used by somebody who has not been
							deactivated, so none was created.
						</p>
				}
			}
		}
		// MONO, BECAUSE THIS IS DATA rather than a heading — the same rule and the
		// same line the transactions section carries. It names the zone the dates
		// inside the dockets are printed in.
		<p class="font-mono text-sm text-ink/85">
			Dates shown in <span class="text-ink">{ v.ZoneLabel }</span>
		</p>
		if v.Actions != nil {
			@components.RosterActions(*v.Actions)
		}
		@components.RosterFilterBar(v.Filters)
		if len(v.People) > 0 {
			<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
				for _, p := range v.People {
					@components.RosterRow(p)
				}
				if v.MoreHref != "" {
					// AN ORDINARY LINK. With no script on this page there is nothing
					// to intercept it, so it navigates — which is also why it says
					// "Next" rather than "Show more": nothing is appended, the list
					// is replaced, and a control naming the wrong behaviour is a
					// control that lies about what pressing it does.
					//
					// THERE IS NO "PREVIOUS", and that is a stated limit rather than
					// an oversight: a keyset cursor walks one way, and the honest
					// backward control is the browser's own Back — every page here
					// is a plain GET with its position in the URL.
					<a class="btn btn--quiet self-start" href={ templ.URL(v.MoreHref) }>Next page</a>
				}
			</div>
		} else if v.Queried {
			@components.EmptyState(emptyRosterHeading(v)) {
				if v.Paged {
					// 🔴 THE POSITION, NOT THE BUSINESS. This branch exists because
					// without it a cursor past the last name printed "No people have
					// been added to this business yet" over a business with staff —
					// measured, from nothing more exotic than a stale bookmark. A
					// page that has run off the end knows only where it is.
					<p>You have reached the end of the list.</p>
					<p class="mt-2">
						<a class="btn btn--quiet" href={ templ.URL(v.StartHref) }>Back to the start</a>
					</p>
				} else if v.Filters.Narrowed {
					<p>Nobody on this list matches those filters. Widen them, or clear them to see everybody.</p>
				} else {
					// 🔴 THIS SENTENCE IS ABOUT THE RECORDS, NOT ABOUT THE BUSINESS.
					// "Nobody works here" would be a claim about the world; what the
					// product actually measured is that this business has no employee
					// rows yet — which is the ordinary state of a tenant on its first
					// day, before anybody has been invited.
					<p>No people have been added to this business yet.</p>
				}
			}
		}
	}
}

// emptyRosterHeading names WHICH claim the empty state is making. The three are
// different and at most one can be true at a time: a page that has run past the last
// name, a business whose people are all filtered out, and a business with nobody on
// the books.
//
// THE CURSOR IS TESTED FIRST, deliberately — and the reason is narrower than the one
// that used to be written here, which claimed both sentences were true.
//
// ⚠️ THEY ARE NOT ALWAYS BOTH TRUE. Paged AND a filter matching nothing at all is a
// real combination, and there "you have reached the end of the list" describes a list
// the reader has never seen a single row of. That is a weaker sentence than it looks
// but it is not a false one: the page IS past the cursor, and the alternative
// ("nobody matches those filters") would send a manager to widen filters that are
// working fine, which is a wrong instruction rather than an imprecise description.
// The cost is bounded by the control beside it — "Back to the start" keeps the
// filters, so the honest view is one click away. Ordering by usefulness rather than
// by truth is the choice, and stating it that way is the point.
func emptyRosterHeading(v EmployeesView) string {
	switch {
	case v.Paged:
		return "That is the end of the list"
	case v.Filters.Narrowed:
		return "Nobody matches these filters"
	default:
		return "Nobody on the books yet"
	}
}

// doneRosterHeading names what the page can SHOW, which is not the same thing as what
// the query string says happened.
//
// 🔴 "Placement saved" WAS AN ACTION CLAIM AND AN AUDIT MEASURED IT UNBACKED:
// `GET /admin/employees?manage=<id>&done=moved`, in a browser that had never posted
// anything, rendered that heading. It is a sendable URL asserting somebody's act —
// exactly the M6-04 defect this section's own comment says it is not repeating, in
// the one branch that was not verified.
//
// THE DEACTIVATED BRANCH IS VERIFIED (the handler suppresses it unless the person the
// card just read IS deactivated, and that is measured going red). "moved" cannot be
// verified the same way: a placement is a STATE and a move is an EVENT, and nothing
// re-reads the previous state. So the heading stops claiming the event and names the
// state instead — a sentence that is true whether the request that produced it was a
// save or a replayed bookmark, and whose facts (venue, department) come from the read
// this very request performed.
//
// ⚠️ WHAT IS LOST, SAID PLAINLY: a manager who presses Save gets a green notice
// naming where the person now works rather than the word "saved". That is a weaker
// receipt and a true one. The alternative — a trail lookup — was weighed and dropped:
// audit_log would confirm that SOME move happened for this employee, not that this
// one just did, so it would buy a stronger-sounding sentence with the same hole.
func doneRosterHeading(done string) string {
	if done == "deactivated" {
		return "Deactivated"
	}
	return "Where they work now"
}

// movedLine says WHERE somebody works, from the card the same request read out of
// the database.
//
// 🔴 IT NAMES THE CURRENT PLACEMENT RATHER THAN A CHANGE. "Moved from the bar to the
// kitchen" would be a claim about a previous state nothing re-reads, and a replayed
// URL would repeat it about a move that never happened. What this prints is a fact
// the server measured in this request, so a stale address can only ever restate
// something true.
func movedLine(a *components.RosterActionsView) string {
	where := a.Venue
	if where == "" {
		where = "a venue that could not be read"
	}
	if a.Department != "" {
		where += " · " + a.Department
	}
	return a.Name + " now works at " + where + "."
}

func problemRosterHeading(problem string) string {
	switch problem {
	case "actions-unavailable":
		return "We could not load this person"
	case "confirm-required":
		return "That deactivation was not confirmed"
	case "confirm-stale":
		return "That confirmation was for somebody else"
	case "unknown":
		return "We could not find that person"
	case "already-deactivated":
		return "Already deactivated"
	case "unknown-placement":
		return "That placement does not fit"
	case "no-change":
		return "Nothing to change"
	case "not-invitable":
		return "No invitation was created"
	default:
		return "We could not read that"
	}
}

// AdminInviteIssued is the ONE screen an activation link is rendered on, and it is
// the response to the POST that minted it — never a page with an address of its own.
//
// 🔴 §4.7 IS THE WHOLE SHAPE OF THIS SCREEN. The link is a live credential: whoever
// holds it can turn this employee's account into a signed-in phone. So it is shown
// as TEXT rather than as a link (a manager clicking it would activate the employee on
// their own browser and put the code in their history), the page is
// Cache-Control: no-store like every other panel response, and there is no way back
// to it — the database keeps only the HMAC, so pressing the button again creates a
// NEW invitation rather than showing this one.
//
// 🔴 IT SAYS WHO IT BELONGS TO. A code handed to the wrong person is an account
// takeover with no forensic difference from a legitimate activation (ADR 0005 Y-D),
// so the name is beside it, at the top, before the credential.
templ AdminInviteIssued(v InviteIssuedView) {
	@PanelShell(v.PanelChrome) {
		@components.Notice(components.ToneOK, "Invitation created for "+v.Name) {
			<p>
				Give this link to { v.Name } — and only to them. Opening it signs their phone
				in, so anybody who reads it can record time as them.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-2">It works for { v.Expires }.</p>
		}
		<section class="docket">
			<p class="docket-label">Activation link — shown once</p>
			// MONO AND BREAKING ON ANY CHARACTER, because this is data that must be
			// copied by eye or by selection. It is deliberately NOT an anchor: see the
			// block above.
			<p class="mt-1 break-all font-mono text-sm text-ink">{ v.ActivationURL }</p>
			<p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink/85">
				Tappa keeps no copy of this link. If you lose this page, create another
				invitation.
			</p>
			// 🔴 THIS SENTENCE HAS BEEN WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS AND IS NOW MEASURED.
			// It first said an earlier link "keeps working until it is used or runs
			// out", which read as "the new one retires the old" and was false. It was
			// replaced by a warning that earlier links are NOT cancelled — true at the
			// time, measured end to end. A user decision (2026-08-08) then made the
			// first reading true for real: issuing retires the employee's pending
			// invitations in the same transaction (migration 00012, cancelled_at).
			// TestPanelEmployeesDB_ASecondInvitationRETIRESTheFirst holds both halves,
			// including the absence of the old warning.
			<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink/85">
				if v.Retired == 1 {
					The previous link for { v.Name } no longer works — creating this one retired
					it. Only this link will sign them in.
				} else if v.Retired > 1 {
					The { itoa(v.Retired) } earlier links for { v.Name } no longer work — creating
					this one retired them. Only this link will sign them in.
				} else {
					This is the only link that will sign { v.Name } in. Creating another one
					later retires this one.
				}
			</p>
		</section>
		<p>
			<a class="btn btn--quiet" href={ templ.URL(v.BackHref) }>Back to the list</a>
		</p>
	}
}
